From: FP6 feature-push (misc agent)
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH RFC] arm64: dts: qcom: milos-fp6: EXPERIMENTAL allow-set-time on PMIC RTC

NOT RECOMMENDED BY DEFAULT. FP6 rtc0 (rtc-pm8xxx, qcom,pmk8350-rtc @0x6100)
reads 1970-01-02 because Linux may not set it (set_time -> -ENODEV without
allow-set-time, nvmem offset or UEFI rtc-info). Android uses time_daemon with
an offset kept in userspace; the staged fp6-rtc-offset service mirrors that.

This RFC enables direct RTC writes for an explicit, bounded RAM-boot test
only (hwclock --systohc once, then compare after a power cycle). Whether the
PMIC accepts HLOS writes on this platform is unverified. The userspace offset
service stays inert whenever rtc0 already reads a plausible (>= 2025) time,
so the two do not double-apply.

Not built or booted.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/milos-fairphone-fp6.dts	2026-10-24 12:48:34.031057301 +0300
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/milos-fairphone-fp6.dts	2026-10-24 12:48:34.082243165 +0300
@@ -1209,6 +1209,18 @@
 	};
 };
 
+&pmk8550_rtc {
+	/*
+	 * EXPERIMENTAL: let Linux write the PMIC RTC directly. Mainline's
+	 * preferred phone-safe alternative is an nvmem "offset" cell in a PMIC
+	 * SDAM (see sc8280xp-crd/sa8540p-ride), but no SDAM byte is known to be
+	 * unused by FP6 firmware (pmk8550_sdam_2 already holds reboot-reason).
+	 * The userspace offset service in nixos-fp6/feature-push/misc is the
+	 * recommended fix; use this only for a bounded RAM-boot write test.
+	 */
+	allow-set-time;
+};
+
 &pmr735b_gpios {
 	s1j_enable_default: s1j-enable-default-state {
 		pins = "gpio1";
